/** A field carrying a standing hint AND, once invalid, an error - both wired to the control by
 *  the contract's own `wiring`, not authored here: `aria-describedby` references both, and
 *  `aria-invalid="true"` follows from `error` being given. The MCP catalogue's own
 *  `form-field-hint-and-error`, verbatim. */
const passwordFieldTree = (translate: Translate): UsageTree => ({
  contract: "form-field",
  signature: "FormField",
  options: { required: true },
  slots: {
    label: translate("demo.passwordField.label"),
    hint: translate("demo.passwordField.hint"),
    error: translate("demo.passwordField.error"),
  },
  children: { contract: "input", signature: "Input", options: { type: "password", controlSize: "md" } },
});

/*
 * The gallery, one step below `showcase`: realistic small pieces of UI, not single components shown
 * in isolation and not whole screens either. Four cards alone cover four different domains - a
 * product (image + text + link), a metric (a number with its trend), a person (an avatar-centered
 * profile with one action), a plan (price + features + one CTA), a file (identity + a menu trigger)
 * - plus a form, a settings row, three alerts, a confirm-before-you-delete modal, a plan picker, a
 * toolbar, an empty state, a trust row, a validated field, and one-tile specimens of the smaller
 * controls (steps, breadcrumb, meter, progress, tag set, shortcut keys, a titled list, a number
 * field, a segmented control, a checkbox group, an accordion, a badge holder, a select, a slider):
 * the normal, expected shapes an app is actually built out of, each one mixing the two-to-five
 * component families that shape genuinely takes. Reused from each family's own docs demo where one
 * already composed the shape (`callout`, `empty-state`, the search-with-button journey, `dialog`'s
 * confirm pattern, the tile radio group, the toolbar, plus the fourteen small-control demos just
 * named); the ten with no demo to borrow (product card, settings row, alert-with-retry, profile
 * row, metric, pricing card, team card, file card, trust row, password field) are composed just
 * above, adapted from the MCP catalogue's own published examples (`get_examples`'s
 * `hero-with-social-proof`, `form-field-hint-and-error` for the two newest; three more are the same
 * shapes applied to a new domain) and checked with `validate_ui` before being trusted here.
 * Thirty-one distinct molecules, into a 5-lane `data-responsive` Grid.
 *
 * Five COLUMNS, not five lanes of one shared Grid: `data-responsive`'s row-based packing (tried
 * first, kept as an intermediate step in this file's own history) always leaves SOME gap under a
 * short tile because every tile in a row still shares one row track - narrowing that gap by
 * sorting, or even closing most of it with fixed-size sub-row tracks, is still fighting the ROW as
 * the unit of layout. A column doesn't have that problem: each of the five is its own `.sk-stack`,
 * tiles poured straight down it, so nothing under a tile is ever bare background - the next tile
 * starts one `sm` gap after the last one ended. Nothing here spans more than one column either
 * (`data-span` doesn't appear below at all): a wide tile would have to pick which column's stack it
 * interrupts, defeating the one thing a column buys.
 *
 * The grid itself sits wider than a normal page section: `data-width="full-width"` (rather than
 * `breakout`, capped at `--size-wrapper-lg`'s 1440px - precisely so a wide viewport still shows
 * five real lanes, per `layout.css`'s own note on that token) puts it on the SAME edge-to-edge
 * track the hero's section already sits on, and `GALLERY_MAX_WIDTH` then caps how far it actually
 * grows - fullbleed with a ceiling, not fullbleed forever, so a 4K monitor gets five wider, airier
 * columns rather than five endless ones. The heading/lede above it shares the same track and cap so
 * their edges line up with the grid's.
 *
 * The five columns aren't equal width either: `GALLERY_COLUMN_WEIGHTS` (`--sk-grid-template`,
 * layout.css) makes the center lane a little wider, its two neighbours equal to each other, and
 * the two outer lanes a little narrower - 319 / 354 / 407 / 354 / 319px at `GALLERY_MAX_WIDTH`.
 * That changes more than proportions: a WIDER column wraps text less, so the same tile measures
 * differently depending which lane it lands in (`settingsRow` alone drops from 121px to 85px
 * simply by moving from a narrow lane to the wide one). Placement below is ORDERED BY that benefit,
 * not by tile identity: the center lane (col 2) holds the tiles that shrink the most as their
 * column widens - `settingsRow`, `tagTonesTree`, `trustRowTree`, `compositionInputButtonTree` -
 * with `productCardTree` pushed to the opposite extreme, a narrow SIDE lane, because it's the one
 * tile that measures WORSE at width (its image's fixed aspect ratio grows taller as its column
 * widens). Everything width-invariant fills in around them wherever a column needs more height.
 *
 * `data-fill` (layout.css) gives all five columns the SAME minimum block-size,
 * `GALLERY_FILL_HEIGHT` - deliberately about double a single screen's worth, so each column reads
 * as a short scroll rather than a fixed panel. It's a FLOOR, not a clamp: the five START level,
 * then a column grows past it and pushes its own lower tiles down when a tile inside it expands
 * (the `accordionExclusiveTree` specimen opening a section, say). Thirty-one distinct molecules
 * cover the resting height; five (`teamCard`, `meter`, `steps`, `metricCard`, `alertRetry`, each
 * repeat-safe: no authored id, no form `name`, no stateful machine) get ONE repeat apiece, with a
 * distinct `idPrefix` - `emitMarkup`'s own escape hatch for independent trees sharing a page, the
 * same machinery that keeps two ColorPickers from portaling into each other - to bring a short
 * column up to that floor. `GALLERY_FILL_HEIGHT` is the tallest of the five resting totals, so the
 * columns start as level as measurement allows.
 *
 * Re-measure every column's total at ITS OWN width (Playwright against the live page,
 * `scrollHeight`, not eyeballed) and move a tile to a shorter column if a tile's content or frame
 * changes height. Measured at `90rem`/1440px (`layout.css`'s own widest `data-columns="5"` tier).
 *
 * Not every tile wears the same frame, either - the rule is: box it, unless it's already a card
 * or a container in its own right. Five molecules - product, metric, pricing, team, file - already
 * build their own `contract:"box"` root (a real card: `raised`/`surface` background, `subtle`
 * border); giving them a second, identical Box around the first just doubles the border and halves
 * the padding's meaning, so their outer `frame` here is bare. Three more - the two Callouts and the
 * retry alert - already carry a full tone-colored surface from `Callout` itself; boxing a
 * box-shaped thing in a second, neutral one muddies the tone rather than framing it, so they're
 * bare too. `toolbarTree` is the same story from a different component: `.sk-toolbar` already
 * paints its own background, border and radius (see `components/toolbar.css`), so an outer card
 * would just be a border around a border. Everything else here has NO surface of its own - a lone
 * button, a form row, a person row, a tile picker, a titled list, the small single-control
 * specimens - so it gets the same `raised`/`subtle` frame the self-boxed tiles' own root already
 * carries: one consistent card language across every tile that isn't already one.
 */
type GalleryFrame = {
  padding: "none" | "sm" | "md" | "lg";
  surface?: "sunken" | "surface" | "raised";
  border?: "subtle" | "default";
};
type GalleryTile = { tree: UsageTree; frame: GalleryFrame; idPrefix?: string };
const box = (padding: GalleryFrame["padding"] = "md"): GalleryFrame => ({ padding, surface: "raised", border: "subtle" });
const bare: GalleryFrame = { padding: "none" };

/** Wider than `breakout`'s own 1440px ceiling (`--size-wrapper-lg`, `layout.css`) on purpose - the
 *  gallery sits in `full-width`'s edge-to-edge track so it can grow past that, capped here instead
 *  of by a named Wrapper step so a 4K monitor doesn't stretch five columns into five ribbons. */
const GALLERY_MAX_WIDTH = 1800;

/** Center lane a little wider, the two flanking it equal to each other, the two outer ones a
 *  little narrower - `--sk-grid-template`'s own escape hatch (layout.css) for weighting a
 *  `data-fill` grid's columns instead of leaving them even. */
const GALLERY_COLUMN_WEIGHTS = "0.9fr 1fr 1.15fr 1fr 0.9fr";

/*
 * THE NEXT TIER UP.
 *
 * `--breakpoint-*` stops at `ultrawide` (90rem) because that is the widest a page column ever gets;
 * this is a GALLERY width, not a page one, so the literal lives here rather than joining the shared
 * map: the 120rem in the `@media` at the foot of this file is a 1920px monitor, the first size
 * where seven lanes have room at all. It is a literal there because a media query cannot read a
 * custom property, the same reason `_breakpoints.scss` exists at all.
 *
 * THE TWO NEW LANES ARE LANES, not a margin decorated with skeletons: they take the SAME weight as
 * the outer real lanes they sit beside (`0.9fr`, the narrow end of `GALLERY_COLUMN_WEIGHTS`), so
 * the seven read as one grid of the same stuff rather than as five columns with two strips glued
 * to the sides. A fixed width was tried first, on the argument that a constant costs the five less
 * than two more `fr` shares do; what it actually bought was two lanes visibly narrower than every
 * card beside them, which made them read as chrome. The cap moves up (`GALLERY_XL_MAX_WIDTH`) so
 * seven lanes of real width have somewhere to go.
 */
const GALLERY_XL_MAX_WIDTH = 2200;

/** The tallest of the five column totals below (px, Chromium/1900, each column's own weighted
 *  share of `GALLERY_MAX_WIDTH`) - roughly double the pre-`data-fill` single-screen height, so
 *  each column is a short scroll of its own. Thirty-one distinct molecules fill most of it; five
 *  get one repeat each (`idPrefix`) to top a column off. */
const GALLERY_FILL_HEIGHT = 1191;

/*
 * THE ULTRAWIDE LANES, and they are skeletons on purpose.
 *
 * Above `ultrawide` the five real lanes stop growing (`GALLERY_MAX_WIDTH`) and the page ends up
 * with two hundred pixels of bare canvas down each side. The honest fix is not a sixth and seventh
 * lane of real molecules: the five are placed by MEASUREMENT (see the note above, each tile in the
 * lane whose width it measures shortest in), and two more lanes of real tiles would be two more
 * columns to re-measure every time any of them changes. Skeletons carry no such debt. They are also
 * the truer thing to put there: the gallery is a window onto a catalogue that does not end at the
 * frame, and the shape of content just past the edge is exactly what a skeleton draws.
 *
 * DIFFERENT SHAPES, not one repeated: a media card, a person, a metric, a paragraph, a list. Each
 * is a composition of `placeholder`'s own signatures, which is the same vocabulary a consumer would
 * reach for while their data is in flight, so these read as loading UI rather than as grey boxes.
 *
 * THE SAME HEIGHT AS EACH OTHER AND AS THE GRID'S FLOOR (`GALLERY_FILL_HEIGHT`), clipped there
 * rather than allowed to end: a skeleton lane that stopped on its own last tile would read as a
 * column that ran out, which is the opposite of the claim. `FadeEdge` takes the bottom of the clip
 * (`sk-fade-edge`, `to-bottom`), so the lane dissolves instead of being cut.
 *
 * `aria-hidden`, like every Placeholder: a picture of absence has nothing to announce, and this one
 * is decoration on top of that.
 */
const skeletonLane = (kinds: readonly SkeletonKind[]): UsageTree[] => kinds.map((kind) => SKELETONS[kind]());

type SkeletonKind = "media" | "person" | "metric" | "paragraph" | "list" | "control";

/*
 * EVERY SKELETON HERE IS STILL (`shimmer: false`), and that is a statement rather than a taste.
 * Nothing in these lanes is loading: they stand in for a catalogue that continues past the frame,
 * so a sweep would be promising an arrival that is never coming. Fourteen shimmering tiles beside
 * the real gallery would also be the loudest thing on a page whose whole subject is the quiet
 * stuff next to them.
 */
const line = (text: string, width: string): UsageTree => ({
  contract: "placeholder",
  signature: "Placeholder",
  options: { text, width, shimmer: false },
});

const SKELETONS: Record<SkeletonKind, () => UsageTree> = {
  /* A product card: image, then its title and two lines under it. */
  media: () => ({
    contract: "layout",
    signature: "Stack",
    options: { gap: "sm" },
    children: [
      { contract: "placeholder", signature: "Placeholder.block", options: { height: "7rem", shimmer: false } },
      line("h4", "70%"),
      { contract: "placeholder", signature: "Placeholder.paragraph", options: { lines: 2, shimmer: false } },
    ],
  }),
  /* A person row: the disc beside two lines, the same shape `profileRowTree` loads into. */
  person: () => ({
    contract: "layout",
    signature: "Inline",
    options: { gap: "sm", inlineAlign: "center" },
    children: [
      { contract: "placeholder", signature: "Placeholder.circle", options: { size: "md", shimmer: false } },
      {
        contract: "layout",
        signature: "Stack",
        options: { gap: "xs" },
        children: [line("body", "8rem"), line("caption", "5rem")],
      },
    ],
  }),
  /* A metric: the number, then what it counts. */
  metric: () => ({
    contract: "layout",
    signature: "Stack",
    options: { gap: "xs" },
    children: [line("caption", "45%"), line("display-sm", "60%")],
  }),
  paragraph: () => ({
    contract: "layout",
    signature: "Stack",
    options: { gap: "sm" },
    children: [line("h5", "55%"), { contract: "placeholder", signature: "Placeholder.paragraph", options: { lines: 4, shimmer: false } }],
  }),
  /* A titled list: a heading over four rows of even length, which is what a list is and a paragraph
     is not (the ragged last line is the difference). */
  list: () => ({
    contract: "layout",
    signature: "Stack",
    options: { gap: "sm" },
    children: [
      line("h5", "40%"),
      ...["85%", "72%", "88%", "64%"].map((width) => line("body", width)),
    ],
  }),
  /* A control row: a label over a field-height block, the shape every form tile loads into. */
  control: () => ({
    contract: "layout",
    signature: "Stack",
    options: { gap: "xs" },
    children: [line("caption", "35%"), { contract: "placeholder", signature: "Placeholder.block", options: { height: "2.5rem", shimmer: false } }],
  }),
};

/*
 * THE ARC, in numbers: how far each lane starts below the top of the row, by distance from the
 * middle. The centre lane leads, the pair beside it drops a step, the outer pair another, and the
 * skeleton lanes at the tier drop one more (`SKELETON_LANE_DROP`), which is what keeps the shape
 * reading as one curve across all seven rather than five staggered lanes with two flat ones stuck
 * on the ends. Read by the stylesheet at the foot of this file, and only from `ultrawide` up.
 */
const LANE_DROPS = ["3rem", "1.5rem", "0rem", "1.5rem", "3rem"];
const SKELETON_LANE_DROP = "4.5rem";

/* How many steps behind the centre lane each one enters, which is just its distance from the
   middle. The skeleton lanes are one step further out again. */
const LANE_STEPS = [2, 1, 0, 1, 2];
const SKELETON_LANE_STEP = 3;

/* Two lanes, each a different reading order, so the pair does not look like one column mirrored. */
const skeletonLanes: UsageTree[][] = [
  skeletonLane(["media", "person", "metric", "list", "control", "paragraph", "person"]),
  skeletonLane(["paragraph", "control", "media", "metric", "person", "list", "metric"]),
];

const columns: GalleryTile[][] = [
  // col 0, 319px (narrow) - productCard leads: the one tile that measures WORSE at width, so it
  // gets the narrowest lane; the rest is width-invariant.
  [
    { tree: productCardTree(t), frame: bare }, // 394
    { tree: meterTree(t), frame: box() }, // 175
    { tree: metricCardTree(t), frame: bare }, // 137
    { tree: calloutInfoTree(t), frame: bare }, // 95
    { tree: badgeHoldersTree(t), frame: box() }, // 76
    { tree: teamCardTree(t), frame: bare, idPrefix: "r1" }, // 211
  ],
  // col 1, 354px (medium)
  [
    { tree: accordionExclusiveTree(t), frame: box() }, // 309
    { tree: stepsTree(t), frame: box() }, // 178
    { tree: alertRetryTree(t), frame: bare }, // 172
    { tree: selectTree(t), frame: box() }, // 100
    { tree: progressTree(t), frame: box() }, // 88
    { tree: kbdTree, frame: box() }, // 54
    { tree: metricCardTree(t), frame: bare, idPrefix: "r1" }, // 137
  ],
  // col 2, 407px (wide centre) - holds every tile that SHRINKS as its column widens:
  // settingsRow, tagTones, trustRow, compositionInputButton all measure shortest here.
  [
    { tree: compositionInputButtonTree(t), frame: box() }, // 105
    { tree: settingsRowTree(t), frame: box() }, // 85
    { tree: tagTonesTree(t), frame: box() }, // 60
    { tree: trustRowTree(t), frame: box() }, // 72
    { tree: emptyStateTree(t), frame: box() }, // 299
    { tree: calloutSuccessTree(t), frame: bare }, // 126
    { tree: dialogConfirmTree(t), frame: box() }, // 76
    { tree: toolbarTree(t), frame: bare }, // 43
    { tree: meterTree(t), frame: box(), idPrefix: "r1" }, // 175
  ],
  // col 3, 354px (medium)
  [
    { tree: listLeadingTree(t), frame: box() }, // 289
    { tree: teamCardTree(t), frame: bare }, // 211
    { tree: passwordFieldTree(t), frame: box() }, // 157
    { tree: numberFieldTree(t), frame: box() }, // 107
    { tree: profileRowTree(t), frame: box() }, // 77
    { tree: breadcrumbTwoTree(t), frame: box() }, // 56
    { tree: stepsTree(t), frame: box(), idPrefix: "r1" }, // 178
  ],
  // col 4, 319px (narrow)
  [
    { tree: pricingCardTree(t), frame: bare }, // 277
    { tree: tileRadioGroupTree(t), frame: box("sm") }, // 224
    { tree: checkboxGroupTree(t), frame: box() }, // 152
    { tree: sliderTree(t), frame: box() }, // 108
    { tree: fileCardTree(t), frame: bare }, // 79
    { tree: segmentedTree(t), frame: box() }, // 64
    { tree: alertRetryTree(t), frame: bare, idPrefix: "r1" }, // 172
  ],
];
---

<Base fullBleed title={t("landing.title")} description={t("landing.description")} hideVanillaMount alwaysShowNavTrigger={false}>
  {/*
    THE PAGE'S OWN LIGHT. Fixed to the viewport rather than sized to a section, because what it is
    lighting is the whole document: the gallery scrolls through it and the grain stays put, so the
    page reads as one surface with the content moving over it rather than as a tinted band with
    ordinary page below. `aria-hidden` and `pointer-events: none`: it is paint, and nothing else.
  */}
  <div class="landing-backdrop" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <div class="sk-layout-grid landing-canvas" style="row-gap: var(--space-stack-xl)">
    {/*
      `breakout` rather than `narrow`: the gallery under it runs the full bleed of the page, and a
      hero at the grid's narrowest measure read as a paragraph someone had left above a wall of
      cards. The band is a `Hero` from the kit now rather than a Box with a stack inside it, for the
      reason this whole page exists: the site does not get to hand-roll a shape the catalogue
      publishes. The headline's `h1` comes from `Heading`'s own `headingSize`, so the one thing lost
      in the move is a hand-written tag name.
    */}
    <section data-width="breakout" class="landing-hero">
      <div class="sk-hero landing-hero__band" data-surface="none" data-padding="xl" data-align="center">
        <div class="sk-stack landing-hero__copy" data-gap="lg" data-align="center">
          <header class="sk-stack" data-gap="md" data-align="center">
            <h1 class="sk-heading" data-size="display-lg" data-flush>{t("landing.hero.title")}</h1>
            <p class="sk-text landing-hero__lede" data-tone="secondary" data-size="lg">{t("landing.hero.lede")}</p>
          </header>
          <div class="sk-inline" data-gap="sm">
            <a class="sk-button sk-interactive" data-sk-button data-tone="accent" data-size="md" href={href("/components")}>
              {t("landing.hero.ctaComponents")}
            </a>
            <a class="sk-button sk-interactive" data-sk-button data-variant="ghost" data-size="md" href={href("/installation")}>
              {t("landing.hero.ctaInstall")}
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-label={t("landing.gallery.title")} data-width="full-width">
      <div
        class="sk-stack"
        data-gap="md"
        data-width="full-width"
        style={`margin-block-end: var(--space-stack-lg); max-width: ${GALLERY_MAX_WIDTH}px; margin-inline: auto; padding-inline: var(--space-inset-lg)`}
      >
        <h2 class="sk-heading" data-size="h2" data-flush>{t("landing.gallery.title")}</h2>
        <p class="sk-text" data-tone="secondary" data-size="lg">{t("landing.gallery.lede")}</p>
      </div>
      <div
        class="sk-grid landing-gallery"
        data-responsive
        data-fill
        data-columns="5"
        data-gap="md"
        data-width="full-width"
        style={`--sk-grid-fill: ${GALLERY_FILL_HEIGHT}px; --sk-grid-template: ${GALLERY_COLUMN_WEIGHTS}; max-width: ${GALLERY_MAX_WIDTH}px; margin-inline: auto; padding-inline: var(--space-inset-lg)`}
      >
        {/*
          The first skeleton lane. Both of them are rendered at every width and revealed only at the
          XL tier (`display: none` below), rather than being emitted conditionally: this page is
          static, so there is no viewport to branch on at build time, and a lane that appears by
          stylesheet cannot disagree with the column template that makes room for it.
        */}
        <div class="landing-gallery__skeleton sk-fade-edge" data-direction="to-bottom" aria-hidden="true" style={`--landing-lane-drop: ${SKELETON_LANE_DROP}; --lane-step: ${SKELETON_LANE_STEP}`}>
          <div class="sk-stack" data-gap="sm">
            {skeletonLanes[0].map((tree, tile) => (
              <div class="sk-box" data-surface="raised" data-border="subtle" data-padding="md" style={`--tile-step: ${tile}`} set:html={emitMarkup(tree, { fillDefaults: false })} />
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>
        {
          columns.map((column, lane) => (
            <div
              class="sk-stack"
              data-gap="sm"
              style={`--landing-lane-drop: ${LANE_DROPS[lane]}; --lane-step: ${LANE_STEPS[lane]}`}
            >
              {column.map(({ tree, frame, idPrefix }, tile) => (
                <div
                  class="sk-box"
                  data-surface={frame.surface}
                  data-border={frame.border}
                  data-padding={frame.padding}
                  style={`--tile-step: ${tile}`}
                  set:html={emitMarkup(tree, { fillDefaults: false, idPrefix })}
                />
              ))}
            </div>
          ))
        }
        <div class="landing-gallery__skeleton sk-fade-edge" data-direction="to-bottom" aria-hidden="true" style={`--landing-lane-drop: ${SKELETON_LANE_DROP}; --lane-step: ${SKELETON_LANE_STEP}`}>
          <div class="sk-stack" data-gap="sm">
            {skeletonLanes[1].map((tree, tile) => (
              <div class="sk-box" data-surface="raised" data-border="subtle" data-padding="md" style={`--tile-step: ${tile}`} set:html={emitMarkup(tree, { fillDefaults: false })} />
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <script is:inline set:html={dialogConfirmScript} />
    </section>

    {/*
      THE CLOSING BAND. `Base` renders `<SiteFooter>` only on railed pages (its own note says why:
      the footer is site chrome that sits beside the document pair), and this page is `fullBleed`,
      so it had none. Rendered here rather than moved into the layout's unconditional branch
      because the two pages want it in different places: a docs page ends after its pager, this one
      ends after the gallery, and `data-width="full-width"` inside this same `sk-layout-grid` is
      what lets the band bleed while its own content returns to the page measure.
    */}
    <SiteFooter nav={navigation} locale={locale} />
  </div>
</Base>

<style define:vars={{
  galleryXlMaxWidth: `${GALLERY_XL_MAX_WIDTH}px`,
  galleryFillHeight: `${GALLERY_FILL_HEIGHT}px`,
}}>
  /*
   * THE BAND'S OWN PAINT, into `--sk-hero-wash`, which is the hook `hero.css` publishes for exactly
   * this. That stylesheet's own note says why it ships flat: a glow lived in the contract briefly
   * and came out again, because decoration is "a call for a consumer's own page to make, not this
   * contract's". This page is that consumer, and this is the one place on the site that gets to be
   * loud.
   *
   * TWO LAYERS, IN THIS ORDER: grain on top, accent light underneath.
   *
   * The light is a wide radial bloom rising from just below the bottom edge, not a top-down linear
   * ramp. A ramp reads as a coloured header band with a seam where it stops; a bloom has no edge to
   * see, so the accent gathers behind the buttons (where the page wants the eye to end up) and is
   * gone by the headline, which therefore keeps full contrast against plain canvas.
   *
   * The grain is `feTurbulence` rendered once into a data URI, tiled at 180px. It is what keeps the
   * bloom from reading as the default soft-gradient-blob every landing page has: at 4% opacity the
   * noise is invisible as texture and visible as a REFUSAL to be a smooth gradient, which is the
   * whole point of asking for grain. `baseFrequency` is high (0.9) so the grain stays fine at any
   * zoom rather than turning into visible blotches on a 4K display.
   *
   * `color-mix` against the accent token rather than a hex: the docs ship a brand switcher, so this
   * band re-tints itself with whatever accent the reader picked, and it goes dark-mode-correct for
   * free because both halves of the mix are already mode-aware.
   */
  /*
   * THE BACKDROP: one wide, shallow field of accent light rising from the foot of the viewport.
   *
   * SHAPED AFTER LUMOS'S OWN, which is the reference asked for and is readable straight out of
   * their stylesheet: `radial-gradient(75% 60% at 50% 88%, brand 14%, transparent 72%)` on a
   * `pointer-events: none` layer, plus a canvas above it masked with
   * `radial-gradient(90% 75% at 50% 92%, #000, #00000080 45%, #0000 78%)`. Three things are worth
   * taking from that and one is worth leaving.
   *
   * WIDE AND LOW, not a disc in the middle. Their ellipse is three quarters of the viewport across
   * and sits at 88% of its height, so the light reads as something the page is standing ON. A
   * centred circle (what this was) reads as a spotlight pointed at the middle of the text, and on a
   * wide screen it is a blob with cold canvas either side of it, which is what "the width of the
   * whole page" was asking to fix.
   *
   * FAINT. Theirs is 14% of the brand colour. This one runs a little warmer at 22% because it also
   * has grain over it, which eats some of the light, but the direction is the same: the field is
   * the room the page sits in, not a thing to look at.
   *
   * MASKED AT THE EDGES, which is the detail that separates this from a gradient someone dropped in
   * a div. The texture fades out before it reaches the sides instead of tiling to a hard stop
   * against them.
   *
   * What is NOT taken: the animated canvas. Their aurora drifts, ours does not. A page whose whole
   * subject is a catalogue of components does not need its background moving behind them, and
   * `prefers-reduced-motion` would have to turn it off anyway.
   */
  .landing-backdrop {
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    overflow: clip;
  }

  /*
   * THE LIGHT, on its own layer so it can move without dragging the grain with it: a transform on
   * the parent would scale the noise tile too, and grain that breathes is grain that reads as a
   * rendering artefact.
   *
   * A CIRCLE, stated as one (`circle <length> at …`) rather than left to a percentage pair, which
   * resolves against the box and therefore comes out an ellipse on every viewport that is not
   * square. The radius is in `rem` so the disc is the same physical size on a laptop and on a
   * 4K panel instead of growing with the window.
   */
  .landing-backdrop::before {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background:
      radial-gradient(
        circle 26rem at 50% 76%,
        color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-action-accent) 34%, transparent) 0%,
        color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-action-accent) 13%, transparent) 52%,
        transparent 78%
      ),
      radial-gradient(
        circle 44rem at 50% 78%,
        color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-action-accent) 20%, transparent) 0%,
        color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-action-accent) 8%, transparent) 48%,
        transparent 76%
      );
    transform-origin: 50% 78%;
    /*
     * THE RESTING STATE, written on the element rather than left to the animation's last frame.
     * Two readers have to land here: the one whose pulse has finished, and the one who never got a
     * pulse because they asked for less motion. Declaring it once is what guarantees those two see
     * the same page rather than the second one seeing whatever the base happened to be.
     */
    opacity: 0.92;
  }

  /*
   * ONE PULSE, ON ARRIVAL. It was looping every nine seconds, which is a light with a heartbeat:
   * fine for two breaths, then it is a thing moving in the corner of the eye for as long as the tab
   * is open, and nothing on the page ever changes in response to it. Once, as the content comes in,
   * says the same thing and then stops saying it.
   *
   * The shape is a lamp coming on rather than a throb: it starts small and dim, overshoots the
   * resting size by a hair, and settles. `both` so it holds that resting frame afterwards instead
   * of snapping back to the declaration above.
   *
   * AN OPT-IN, not an override. A reader who asked for reduced motion never enters this block, so
   * they get the static light declared above, and so does anyone whose browser the query cannot
   * read. Same for the gallery's reveal further down: the tiles animate inside a
   * `no-preference` block and are simply present outside it.
   */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .landing-backdrop::before {
      animation: landing-pulse 2.4s var(--scale-easing-enter, cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1)) both;
    }
  }

  /*
   * Scale and opacity move together throughout: scaling alone reads as the window resizing, fading
   * alone reads as a flicker, and the two together read as light.
   */
  @keyframes landing-pulse {
    from {
      transform: scale(0.92);
      opacity: 0.4;
    }
    60% {
      transform: scale(1.05);
      opacity: 1;
    }
    to {
      transform: scale(1);
      opacity: 0.92;
    }
  }

  /*
   * THE REVEAL: one orchestrated moment on load, ordered from the middle of the gallery outward.
   *
   * TWO STAGGERS NESTED. The lanes go first, by distance from the centre (`--lane-step`, written on
   * each lane), so the middle column arrives, then the pair beside it, then the outer pair, then the
   * skeleton lanes at the tier. Inside each lane the tiles follow on their own step
   * (`--tile-step`), so a column fills downward rather than appearing whole. The delay each tile
   * actually waits is its lane's head start plus its own position, which is one multiplication in
   * the markup and nothing for the stylesheet to work out.
   *
   * It agrees with the arc the lanes already sit in: the centre lane starts highest AND first, the
   * outer ones start lowest AND last, so the eye is pulled along the same curve twice.
   *
   * RISE AND FADE, 8px. Far enough to be a direction, close enough that a reader who blinks has
   * missed nothing: the page is readable at rest and the motion is how it got there, not a thing to
   * watch. The whole sequence is over in under a second at the furthest tile.
   *
   * `backwards` fill so a tile waiting its turn is already invisible rather than flashing at full
   * opacity for the length of its own delay.
   */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    /* Both lane shapes: a real lane IS the stack, a skeleton lane WRAPS one (it needs the fade-edge
       element around it), so its tiles sit a level deeper. Selecting `> * > *` alone reached the
       stack inside the skeleton lane rather than the tiles in it, and those never animated. */
    .landing-gallery > .sk-stack > *,
    .landing-gallery > .landing-gallery__skeleton > .sk-stack > * {
      animation: landing-rise var(--motion-enter-duration, 240ms) var(--motion-enter-easing, ease-out)
        both;
      animation-delay: calc(var(--lane-step, 0) * 90ms + var(--tile-step, 0) * 55ms);
    }
  }

  @keyframes landing-rise {
    from {
      opacity: 0;
      transform: translateY(8px);
    }
    to {
      opacity: 1;
      transform: none;
    }
  }

  /* Everything the page renders sits over the light, in its own layer. */
  .landing-canvas {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
  }

  /* A reader who asked for less contrast keeps the light and loses the texture. */
  @media (prefers-contrast: more) {
    .landing-backdrop::after {
      display: none;
    }
  }

  /*
   * THE OPENING SCREEN. The band takes most of the first viewport and centres its copy in what it
   * takes, so the page opens on the sentence rather than on the sentence plus a strip of gallery
   * peeking in under it. `min()` rather than a flat `vh`: on a short laptop window 78vh is the
   * right share of the screen, and on a tall monitor a hero that keeps growing with the glass is
   * just a bigger empty room, so 42rem caps it.
   *
   * `justify-content: center` is already `hero.css`'s own doing (it centres its single child
   * unconditionally, see its note on the min-height), which is why raising the floor is all this
   * takes to put the copy in the middle of the screen.
   */
  .landing-hero__band {
    --sk-hero-min-height: min(78vh, 42rem);
  }

  /*
   * TYPE THAT SCALES WITH THE PAGE. `display-lg` is the largest step the scale publishes, and it is
   * sized for a page heading inside a document, not for the one line a landing page opens on: at
   * `breakout` width it filled about half the measure and read as a section title that happened to
   * be first. `clamp` takes the token as its floor, so nothing gets SMALLER than the scale on a
   * phone, and lets it grow with the viewport up to 5.25rem.
   *
   * The lede moves with it, a step under `lg`'s own size: a headline that grows while the line
   * under it stays put stops reading as a pair.
   */
  .landing-hero__copy .sk-heading {
    font-size: clamp(var(--font-size-display-lg), 5.2vw, 5.25rem);
    line-height: 1.04;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }

  .landing-hero__lede {
    /*
     * A CENTRED LINE NEEDS A MEASURE more than a ranged-left one does: with no reliable left edge
     * to return to, a long centred line is read by hunting for where the next one starts. Capped at
     * 42ch and centred in the band.
     */
    max-inline-size: 42ch;
    margin-inline: auto;
    text-align: center;
    font-size: clamp(var(--font-size-body-lg, 1.125rem), 1.35vw, 1.5rem);
  }

  .landing-hero__copy {
    text-align: center;
  }

  /*
   * The skeleton lanes are laid out by this sheet alone; the grid itself keeps its five-lane inline
   * style at every width below the tier. `display: none` rather than `visibility` or opacity: a
   * hidden-but-present lane would still claim a grid track, and below the tier the template has no
   * track to give it, so the lane would wrap into a row of its own under the gallery.
   */
  .landing-gallery__skeleton {
    display: none;
  }

  /*
   * THE ARC. The lanes do not all start at the same y: the centre one sits highest, its two
   * neighbours drop a step, the outer pair drops another, and at the tier the skeleton lanes drop
   * one more. Five identical tops read as a wall, and the eye lands on the whole block at once
   * instead of on anything in it; a shallow arc gives it an entry point (the middle, which is also
   * the widest lane) and a direction to leave by.
   *
   * `margin-block-start`, not `translate`: the offset has to COST height, or the lanes at the
   * bottom of the arc would hang past the section while the grid still believed everything ended
   * level. Margin pushes the grid's own box down with them.
   *
   * ONLY FROM `ultrawide` UP, where all five lanes are genuinely side by side. Below it the grid
   * falls to four, three, two and finally one lane (`data-responsive`), and an offset keyed to a
   * lane's position in a five-lane row becomes a random gap above a stacked column.
   *
   * WHICH lane gets which step is written on the lane itself (`--landing-lane-drop`, `LANE_DROPS`
